    "evidence": [
      {
        "evidence_id": "E001",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "Appeal from an order of the Supreme Court (Krogmann, J.), entered May 14, 2014 in Warren County, which granted defendants’ motions for summary judgment dismissing the complaint.",
        "statement_status": "procedural_history"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E002",
        "passage_id": "P005",
        "quote": "Ordered that the order is reversed, on the law, with costs, and motions denied.",
        "statement_status": "current_disposition"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E003",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "Plaintiff rented an apartment in a building owned by defendant Jeffrey Hiscox and managed on behalf of defendant Hometown Real Estate by defendant Andrew Martin. The building has an exterior back porch accessible from the first floor apartments and, in October 2011, plaintiff slipped and fell on steps leading down from it. Plaintiff then commenced this premises liability action, alleging that her fall was caused by a slippery green substance on the porch that could and should have been addressed by defendants.",
        "statement_status": "allegation"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E004",
        "passage_id": "P002",
        "quote": "Plaintiff was required by the lease to “properly care for the” porch area by keeping it clear of debris and snow, but it is doubtful that her limited duties in that regard absolved de *1127 fendants of their general responsibility to maintain a seemingly common area accessible by other tenants (see Hamelin v Town of Chateaugay, 100 AD3d 1330, 1331 [2012]; Wynn v T.R.I.P. Redevelopment Assoc., 296 AD2d 176, 179 [2002]). Moreover, even assuming that the porch was not a common area, defendants reserved the right to enter the premises leased by plaintiff for inspection and repair purposes and, as such, may be held “liable for failure to repair a dangerous condition” therein if they were on notice of it",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E005",
        "passage_id": "P003",
        "quote": "Here, Martin and Hiscox were both aware that there was “green slime” on the porch around the time that plaintiff rented the apartment in May 2011. Hiscox authorized Martin to hire a contractor to clean the porch, which was done, and Martin stated that he did not observe the substance on his subsequent visits to the property. Martin and Hiscox, moreover, both denied receiving any complaints about the condition of the porch after that time.",
        "statement_status": "testimony_or_record"
